#ASSERT `Now that my partner has moved out, do the deferred charges on our home become payable` `as the buyer or executor` `an unasked partner event` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` EQUALS `does not arise for you`

§§ `For a person buying, inheriting, lending on or administering a home with deferred charges (ss 44(2), 45, 46, 48, 49)`

-- Are the unpaid deferred charges a charge on this land?
#ASSERT `Are the unpaid deferred charges a charge on this land` `as the buyer or executor` EQUALS yes
#ASSERT `Are the unpaid deferred charges a charge on this land` `as the ratepayer` EQUALS `does not arise for you`
#ASSERT `Are the unpaid deferred charges a charge on this land` `as the partner` EQUALS `does not arise for you`

-- How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land?
-- s 46(1): "before any other kind of charge".
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the buyer or executor` `a mortgage or other charge not created by an Act` EQUALS `ranks` `ahead of it`
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the buyer or executor` `another charge created by an Act` EQUALS `ranks` `equally with it`
-- s 46(2).
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the buyer or executor` `an amount owed to the Housing Authority under the housing Acts or a housing scheme` EQUALS `ranks` `behind it`
-- s 46(3).
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the buyer or executor` `an amount owed to the Defence Homes Corporation` EQUALS `ranks` `behind it`
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the ratepayer` `an unasked claim` EQUALS `does not arise for you`
#ASSERT `How do the deferred charges rank against this other claim on the land` `as the partner` `an unasked claim` EQUALS `does not arise for you`

-- Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered?
-- k095: Jake's estate; he has no surviving partner.
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the buyer or executor` `the pensioner died` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS yes
-- s 45(1).
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the buyer or executor` `the pensioner died` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS no
-- s 44(2)(b): a sale.
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the buyer or executor` `the home stopped being the pensioner's, for example it was sold` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS yes
-- s 44(2)(c)(i).
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the buyer or executor` `the pensioner stopped living there` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS no
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the buyer or executor` `the pensioner stopped living there` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS yes
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the ratepayer` `an unasked happening` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` EQUALS `does not arise for you`
#ASSERT `Have the deferred charges become payable, so that they may be recovered` `as the partner` `an unasked happening` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` `an unasked fact` EQUALS `does not arise for you`

